.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "parseArgs"
.PP
\fR\fP
.PP
Polyfill of \fRutil\&.parseArgs()\fP
.SS "\fRutil\&.parseArgs([config])\fP"
.RS 4
Stability: 1 - Experimental 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRconfig\fP {Object} Used to provide arguments for parsing and to configure the parser\&. \fRconfig\fP supports the following properties:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRargs\fP {string[]} array of argument strings\&. \fBDefault:\fP \fRprocess\&.argv\fP with \fRexecPath\fP and \fRfilename\fP removed\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRoptions\fP {Object} Used to describe arguments known to the parser\&. Keys of \fRoptions\fP are the long names of options and values are an {Object} accepting the following properties:
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fRtype\fP {string} Type of argument, which must be either \fRboolean\fP or \fRstring\fP\&.
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fRmultiple\fP {boolean} Whether this option can be provided multiple times\&. If \fRtrue\fP, all values will be collected in an array\&. If \fRfalse\fP, values for the option are last-wins\&. \fBDefault:\fP \fRfalse\fP\&.
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fRshort\fP {string} A single character alias for the option\&.
.IP "    \(bu" 6
\fRdefault\fP {string | boolean | string[] | boolean[]} The default option value when it is not set by args\&. It must be of the same type as the the \fRtype\fP property\&. When \fRmultiple\fP is \fRtrue\fP, it must be an array\&.
.PP

.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRstrict\fP {boolean} Should an error be thrown when unknown arguments are encountered, or when arguments are passed that do not match the \fRtype\fP configured in \fRoptions\fP\&. \fBDefault:\fP \fRtrue\fP\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRallowPositionals\fP {boolean} Whether this command accepts positional arguments\&. \fBDefault:\fP \fRfalse\fP if \fRstrict\fP is \fRtrue\fP, otherwise \fRtrue\fP\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRtokens\fP {boolean} Return the parsed tokens\&. This is useful for extending the built-in behavior, from adding additional checks through to reprocessing the tokens in different ways\&. \fBDefault:\fP \fRfalse\fP\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Returns: {Object} The parsed command line arguments:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRvalues\fP {Object} A mapping of parsed option names with their {string} or {boolean} values\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRpositionals\fP {string[]} Positional arguments\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRtokens\fP {Object[] | undefined} See \fBparseArgs tokens\fP section\&. Only returned if \fRconfig\fP includes \fRtokens: true\fP\&.
.PP

.PP

.PP
Provides a higher level API for command-line argument parsing than interacting with \fRprocess\&.argv\fP directly\&. Takes a specification for the expected arguments and returns a structured object with the parsed options and positionals\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { parseArgs } from 'node:util';
const args = ['\-f', '\-\-bar', 'b'];
const options = {
  foo: {
    type: 'boolean',
    short: 'f'
  },
  bar: {
    type: 'string'
  }
};
const {
  values,
  positionals
} = parseArgs({ args, options });
console\&.log(values, positionals);
// Prints: [Object: null prototype] { foo: true, bar: 'b' } []
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const { parseArgs } = require('node:util');
const args = ['\-f', '\-\-bar', 'b'];
const options = {
  foo: {
    type: 'boolean',
    short: 'f'
  },
  bar: {
    type: 'string'
  }
};
const {
  values,
  positionals
} = parseArgs({ args, options });
console\&.log(values, positionals);
// Prints: [Object: null prototype] { foo: true, bar: 'b' } []
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fRutil\&.parseArgs\fP is experimental and behavior may change\&. Join the conversation in \fRpkgjs/parseargs\fP to contribute to the design\&.
.SS "\fRparseArgs\fP \fRtokens\fP"
Detailed parse information is available for adding custom behaviours by specifying \fRtokens: true\fP in the configuration\&. The returned tokens have properties describing:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
all tokens
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRkind\fP {string} One of 'option', 'positional', or 'option-terminator'\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRindex\fP {number} Index of element in \fRargs\fP containing token\&. So the source argument for a token is \fRargs[token\&.index]\fP\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
option tokens
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRname\fP {string} Long name of option\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRrawName\fP {string} How option used in args, like \fR-f\fP of \fR--foo\fP\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRvalue\fP {string | undefined} Option value specified in args\&. Undefined for boolean options\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRinlineValue\fP {boolean | undefined} Whether option value specified inline, like \fR--foo=bar\fP\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
positional tokens
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRvalue\fP {string} The value of the positional argument in args (i\&.e\&. \fRargs[index]\fP)\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
option-terminator token
.PP

.PP
The returned tokens are in the order encountered in the input args\&. Options that appear more than once in args produce a token for each use\&. Short option groups like \fR-xy\fP expand to a token for each option\&. So \fR-xxx\fP produces three tokens\&.

.PP
For example to use the returned tokens to add support for a negated option like \fR--no-color\fP, the tokens can be reprocessed to change the value stored for the negated option\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { parseArgs } from 'node:util';

const options = {
  'color': { type: 'boolean' },
  'no\-color': { type: 'boolean' },
  'logfile': { type: 'string' },
  'no\-logfile': { type: 'boolean' },
};
const { values, tokens } = parseArgs({ options, tokens: true });

// Reprocess the option tokens and overwrite the returned values\&.
tokens
  \&.filter((token) => token\&.kind === 'option')
  \&.forEach((token) => {
    if (token\&.name\&.startsWith('no\-')) {
      // Store foo:false for \-\-no\-foo
      const positiveName = token\&.name\&.slice(3);
      values[positiveName] = false;
      delete values[token\&.name];
    } else {
      // Resave value so last one wins if both \-\-foo and \-\-no\-foo\&.
      values[token\&.name] = token\&.value ?? true;
    }
  });

const color = values\&.color;
const logfile = values\&.logfile ?? 'default\&.log';

console\&.log({ logfile, color });
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const { parseArgs } = require('node:util');

const options = {
  'color': { type: 'boolean' },
  'no\-color': { type: 'boolean' },
  'logfile': { type: 'string' },
  'no\-logfile': { type: 'boolean' },
};
const { values, tokens } = parseArgs({ options, tokens: true });

// Reprocess the option tokens and overwrite the returned values\&.
tokens
  \&.filter((token) => token\&.kind === 'option')
  \&.forEach((token) => {
    if (token\&.name\&.startsWith('no\-')) {
      // Store foo:false for \-\-no\-foo
      const positiveName = token\&.name\&.slice(3);
      values[positiveName] = false;
      delete values[token\&.name];
    } else {
      // Resave value so last one wins if both \-\-foo and \-\-no\-foo\&.
      values[token\&.name] = token\&.value ?? true;
    }
  });

const color = values\&.color;
const logfile = values\&.logfile ?? 'default\&.log';

console\&.log({ logfile, color });
.fi
.PP

.PP
Example usage showing negated options, and when an option is used multiple ways then last one wins\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ node negate\&.js
{ logfile: 'default\&.log', color: undefined }
$ node negate\&.js \-\-no\-logfile \-\-no\-color
{ logfile: false, color: false }
$ node negate\&.js \-\-logfile=test\&.log \-\-color
{ logfile: 'test\&.log', color: true }
$ node negate\&.js \-\-no\-logfile \-\-logfile=test\&.log \-\-color \-\-no\-color
{ logfile: 'test\&.log', color: false }
.fi
.PP

