.SS "F\&.A\&.Qs"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Is \fRcmd --foo=bar baz\fP the same as \fRcmd baz --foo=bar\fP?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
yes
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does the parser execute a function?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does the parser execute one of several functions, depending on input?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Can subcommands take options that are distinct from the main command?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does it output generated help when no options match?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does it generated short usage? Like: \fRusage: ls [-ABCFGHLOPRSTUWabcdefghiklmnopqrstuwx1] [file \&.\&.\&.]\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no (no usage/help at all)
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does the user provide the long usage text? For each option? For the whole command?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Do subcommands (if implemented) have their own usage output?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does usage print if the user runs \fRcmd --help\fP?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does it set \fRprocess\&.exitCode\fP?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does usage print to stderr or stdout?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
N/A
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does it check types? (Say, specify that an option is a boolean, number, etc\&.)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Can an option have more than one type? (string or false, for example)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Can the user define a type? (Say, \fRtype: path\fP to call \fRpath\&.resolve()\fP on the argument\&.)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does a \fR--foo=0o22\fP mean 0, 22, 18, or "0o22"?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR"0o22"\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does it coerce types?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does \fR--no-foo\fP coerce to \fR--foo=false\fP? For all options? Only boolean options?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no, it sets \fR{values:{'no-foo': true}}\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Is \fR--foo\fP the same as \fR--foo=true\fP? Only for known booleans? Only at the end?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no, they are not the same\&. There is no special handling of \fRtrue\fP as a value so it is just another string\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does it read environment variables? Ie, is \fRFOO=1 cmd\fP the same as \fRcmd --foo=1\fP?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Do unknown arguments raise an error? Are they parsed? Are they treated as positional arguments?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no, they are parsed, not treated as positionals
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does \fR--\fP signal the end of options?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
yes
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Is \fR--\fP included as a positional?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Is \fRprogram -- foo\fP the same as \fRprogram foo\fP?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
yes, both store \fR{positionals:['foo']}\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Does the API specify whether a \fR--\fP was present/relevant?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Is \fR-bar\fP the same as \fR--bar\fP?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no, \fR-bar\fP is a short option or options, with expansion logic that follows the \fRUtility Syntax Guidelines in POSIX\&.1-2017\fP\&. \fR-bar\fP expands to \fR-b\fP, \fR-a\fP, \fR-r\fP\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Is \fR---foo\fP the same as \fR--foo\fP?
.IP "  \(bu" 4
no
.IP "  \(bu" 4
the first is a long option named \fR'-foo'\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
the second is a long option named \fR'foo'\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Is \fR-\fP a positional? ie, \fRbash some-test\&.sh | tap -\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
yes
.PP

